  S~S This Repository

------------------------------------------

  S~S  What is the purpose of the software in this repository?

    * Support SimpleSal ecosystem educational goals by providing free software source code.
    * Provide software of moderate complexity that can be broken, fixed, improved, abandoned.
    * Demonstrate the tradeoffs of performing software engineering tasks of moderate complexity.

------------------------------------------   [edit: this published thing will look like this]

  S~S  What are the contents of this repository?

   * While accessed through the repo App, the contents being worked on ARE the managed object.
     NOT "in the managed object" BUT "are the managed object": as a directory, or, a set of files.

   * Once transported within the workspace from the repo through the medium to the instance,
     the managed object becomes "the specific contents of the managed object being modified".

------------------------------------------

  S~S  What happens in my workspace as a result of interacting with this repository?

  * The instance in the workspace contains a copy of the contents of the managed object.

    * for a non-SimpleSal App:   'the contents are placed into a directory with the name required
                                 by the developer's build; for Arduino, the ".ino" file name.'

    * for a SimpleSal App:       'the contents..., a file named App.ino.'   Same as non-SimpleSal.

    * for the SimpleSal source:  'the contents..., a directory, goes into the App as rr\App\SimpleSal\'
                                  See notes below: advanced local source code version management.

  * The SimpleSal and TSC LLC philosophy about organizing software in a workspace over Time
    is related to the principle some call "separation of concerns" in software development:

        any version of a SimpleSal App should work with any version of SimpleSal.

    The contents of the App's directory and the contents of SimpleSal are mutually exclusive.
       The result: App repos have no SimpleSal directory, a SimpleSal repo has no App directory.

------------------------------------------

=====================

------------------------------------------   [edit: provide a view from 10,000 feet and falling]

  S~S   directory tree structure:

  User space for Arduino is a directory, "App", containing the "SimpleSal" directory, and "App.ino".
  The Arduino IDE build environment expects a SimpleSal directory to be in each App's directory.

  The \SimpleSal\ directory contains all of the software source code needed to use SimpleSal.
  Three files in the \SimpleSal\ directory are copied to the App directory and renamed.

      the "ssTEA" subdirectory contains the software implementing Time and Event Agency (TEA).

      the "ssUI" subdirectory contains the menu system that displays Time and Event information.

      the "ssDocs" subdirectory contains documentation, each file is pointed to by source code files.

      the "ssIO" subdirectory is independent of ssTEA and implements the virtualization of hardware.

------------------------------------------

=====================                           [edit: truth is often buried in the notes]

------------------------------------------

  S~S   Project notes and other information that is not easy to place in a category

------------------------------------------

  C-language source code at a "fundamental Kernighan and Ritchie C level"; also called "K and R C".

  C software is implemented to directly translate to processor-level instructions and capabilities.
  SimpleSal is also going to be integrated into other languages and Hosts; we must begin at simple.

  There are experts within each software development ecosystem that know how to reference C software.
  The author of SimpleSal has no expertise in any of those cultures; you don't want him making dinner.

------------------------------------------

  'advanced local source code version management'

  The SimpleSal App references the version of SimpleSal indicated by the software build.

  The SimpleSal model using the Arduino out-of-the-box build environment is \App\SimpleSal\.
  This shows the include path used by the software build determines where \SimpleSal\ lives.

  It is common to reach outside the App's narrow workspace and reference software "out there".

  The still-awake reader will realize that a single cloned instance of the SimpleSal/simplesal
  repo managed object creates a directory at the same level as the \App\ directories.

    This instance of the managed object may be copied to App directories or referenced here.
    On a resume, the choice made is described as 'advanced local source code version management'.

------------------------------------------

  SimpleSal'S assistance with initial configuration is based in files with the extension ".start".
  To begin, these files must be copied into the App user space and renamed as ".h" files.
  The files do not neeed to be changed; the directions for integration into the App expect ".h".

  The expectation is that the normal user will extend basic Mesa abilities and reconfigure ssTEA.

------------------------------------------

  There is ssDocs documentation specific to the architecture and implementation of ssTEA and ssUI,
  as software components co-existing with the App.  For example, allocation of the database used
  for Event descriptors may be performed by the App or ssTEA; the App is free to take control and
  replace ssTEA's static allocation algorithm with a dynamic allocation using malloc.  Try it.

